{
  "text": " This is how large language models work, explained in under 60 seconds. LLMs like chat GPT and Clots start with massive amounts of data collection. We're talking about scraping nearly the entire internet, books, articles, code, social media posts, you name it. Your prompt isn't processed as words, but they're broken down into tokens using the tokenizer. These are smaller chunks like syllables or individual characters that are then converted into numbers, because keep in mind, machines don't understand words. They only understand numbers. After the tokenizer does this, the embeddings are generated, which is like a matrix of numbers, which represents the semantic meaning of the word in a high-dimensional vector space. Now, these embeddings, they flow through billions of artificial neurons in something called a transformer architecture. Each layer of neurons specialize in understanding different aspects of a sentence, from basic grammar to complex reasoning. The real magic happens in what's called the attention mechanism. As each word in your prompt is processed, the model pays attention to relevant words that came before it. Then the model predicts next token based on statistical patterns it learned during the training, which we showed at the top. For context, the training data is so vast that for a human to read everything GPT-3 was trained on, keeping mind with it in GPT-4, it would take 2600 years reading non-stop 24-7. For a person to read, that's insane. This prediction process repeats token by token until you get a complete response, with each new word influenced by everything that came before it. Now, this is why you usually see those text animations in chat GPT and cloud, where instead of getting a single text output all at once, text is generated word by word in real time, they probably use some kind of HTML or CSS trick to keep rendering these new words, but that's usually why you see those animations of words appearing bit by bit, or the sentences being constructed word by word. Follow me for more.",
